k/css_is_still_awesome"},{"short_id":"51zi5s","created_at":"2026-08-31T12:16:18.006-05:00","title":"What is the real maximum length of a DNS name? (2012)","url":"https://devblogs.microsoft.com/oldnewthing/20120412-00/?p=7873","score":3,"flags":0,"comment_count":1,"description":"","description_plain":"","submitter_user":"Halkcyon","user_is_author":false,"tags":["networking"],"short_id_url":"https://lobste.rs/s/51zi5s","comments_url":"https://lobste.rs/s/51zi5s/what_is_real_maximum_length_dns_name_2012"},{"short_id":"xygw0y","created_at":"2026-08-31T12:03:47.803-05:00","title":"Bootstrappable builds: how and why","url":"https://lwn.net/Articles/1088279/","score":10,"flags":0,"comment_count":3,"description":"","description_plain":"","submitter_user":"pushcx","user_is_author":false,"tags":["lisp","nix"],"short_id_url":"https://lobste.rs/s/xygw0y","comments_url":"https://lobste.rs/s/xygw0y/bootstrappable_builds_how_why"},{"short_id":"hyn2bt","created_at":"2026-08-31T11:53:28.422-05:00","title":"RangeFrom, Part 2..: What I think is wrong about the design","url":"https://erk.dev/2026/08/30/rangefrom-part-2","score":12,"flags":0,"comment_count":9,"description":"","description_plain":"","submitter_user":"valdemar","user_is_author":true,"tags":["rust"],"short_id_url":"https://lobste.rs/s/hyn2bt","comments_url":"https://lobste.rs/s/hyn2bt/rangefrom_part_2_what_i_think_is_wrong"},{"short_id":"77kss6","created_at":"2026-08-31T11:39:11.268-05:00","title":"Data Became Code: We Ran Code Inside Fortune 500s Using Files They Published for AI Agents","url":"https://medium.com/@alonhertz1/data-became-code-we-ran-code-inside-fortune-500s-using-files-they-published-for-ai-agents-0cd67ffbbffc","score":0,"flags":2,"comment_count":1,"description":"","description_plain":"","submitter_user":"btp","user_is_author":false,"tags":["ai","security"],"short_id_url":"https://lobste.rs/s/77kss6","comments_url":"https://lobste.rs/s/77kss6/data_became_code_we_ran_code_inside"},{"short_id":"riufop","created_at":"2026-08-31T11:16:51.364-05:00","title":"Shrimply: open source video editor with GPU path tracing and Manim integration","url":"https://github.com/soirihiroka/shrimply","score":3,"flags":0,"comment_count":1,"description":"\u003cp\u003edevlog: \u003ca href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=IjMI086l9Mo\" rel=\"ugc\"\u003ehttps://www.youtube.com/watch?v=IjMI086l9Mo\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n","description_plain":"devlog: https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=IjMI086l9Mo","submitter_user":"olympicene","user_is_author":false,"tags":["graphics","rust","video"],"short_id_url":"https://lobste.rs/s/riufop","comments_url":"https://lobste.rs/s/riufop/shrimply_open_source_video_editor_with"},{"short_id":"qn7jtv","created_at":"2026-08-31T10:22:00.683-05:00","title":"I attended a conference recently and AI use by academics was absurd","url":"","score":74,"flags":1,"comment_count":28,"description":"\u003cp\u003eAs we listened to talks most people were stuck doing pointless tasks on their phones and computers. Less than 10%, likely 5%, were listening attentively. This was quite sad for me.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe thing I would like to share the most, that shocked me more than any AI use I've seen ever—when I was on stage receiving QA post talk among the fellow panelists, everyone but me used AI on their laptops to record their questions and then come up with a response they could use. IDK about others, but I spent a lot of time on my talk and slideshow. The professor who served as the respondent listened for two hours taking a ton of notes then formed thoughtful questions. I cannot believe the laziness and disrespect to the professor's intellect for all the other panelists to rely on AI for their response.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eFor me, the seduction of AI to do boring tasks such as translation, CV writing, and job applications, and editing papers is forever there but I choose to believe these tasks benefit me as someone trying to be a so-called intellectual. To see everyone around me choose to not think for themselves, when we presenters should be the most knowledgeable in the room about our topics, shocked me beyond anything I've seen in academic.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eI am constantly considering the use of AI in the classroom and research and thought this reflection/rant would be relevant here.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eI'm curious if my fellow Lobsters have seen similar uses of AI or what their opinions are.\u003c/p\u003e\n","description_plain":"As we listened to talks most people were stuck doing pointless tasks on their phones and computers. Less than 10%, likely 5%, were listening attentively. This was quite sad for me.\r\n\r\nThe thing I would like to share the most, that shocked me more than any AI use I've seen ever—when I was on stage receiving QA post talk among the fellow panelists, everyone but me used AI on their laptops to record their questions and then come up with a response they could use. IDK about others, but I spent a lot of time on my talk and slideshow. The professor who served as the respondent listened for two hours taking a ton of notes then formed thoughtful questions. I cannot believe the laziness and disrespect to the professor's intellect for all the other panelists to rely on AI for their response.\r\n\r\nFor me, the seduction of AI to do boring tasks such as translation, CV writing, and job applications, and editing papers is forever there but I choose to believe these tasks benefit me as someone trying to be a so-called intellectual. To see everyone around me choose to not think for themselves, when we presenters should be the most knowledgeable in the room about our topics, shocked me beyond anything I've seen in academic. \r\n\r\nI am constantly considering the use of AI in the classroom and research and thought this reflection/rant would be relevant here. \r\n\r\nI'm curious if my fellow Lobsters have seen similar uses of AI or what their opinions are.","submitter_user":"oceanhaiyang","user_is_author":true,"tags":["rant","vibecoding"],"short_id_url":"https://lobste.rs/s/qn7jtv","comments_url":"https://lobste.rs/s/qn7jtv/i_attended_conference_recently_ai_use_by"},{"short_id":"49hbhi","created_at":"2026-08-31T09:19:48.353-05:00","title":"Cancelation Terminology","url":"https://matklad.github.io/2026/08/31/cancelation-terminology.html","score":19,"flags":0,"comment_count":0,"description":"","description_plain":"","submitter_user":"vulcan","user_is_author":false,"tags":["programming"],"short_id_url":"https://lobste.rs/s/49hbhi","comments_url":"https://lobste.rs/s/49hbhi/cancelation_terminology"},{"short_id":"vb1jgu","created_at":"2026-08-31T09:06:11.119-05:00","title":"What are you doing this week?","url":"","score":8,"flags":0,"comment_count":22,"description":"\u003cp\u003eWhat are you doing this week? Feel free to share!\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eKeep in mind it’s OK to do nothing at all, too.\u003c/p\u003e\n","description_plain":"What are you doing this week? Feel free to share!\r\n\r\nKeep in mind it’s OK to do nothing at all, too.","submitter_user":"caius","user_is_author":true,"tags":["ask","programming"],"short_id_url":"https://lobste.rs/s/vb1jgu","comments_url":"https://lobste.rs/s/vb1jgu/what_are_you_doing_this_week"},{"short_id":"q2zziq","created_at":"2026-08-31T08:46:41.291-05:00","title":"Any user process can escalate to root","url":"https://www.vesto.me/2026/08/31/any-process-escalate-root.html","score":8,"flags":1,"comment_count":2,"description":"","description_plain":"","submitter_user":"ntietz","user_is_author":false,"tags":["security"],"short_id_url":"https://lobste.rs/s/q2zziq","comments_url":"https://lobste.rs/s/q2zziq/any_user_process_can_escalate_root"},{"short_id":"uxqdfr","created_at":"2026-08-31T08:33:40.389-05:00","title":"On not becoming a cyborg","url":"https://nolanlawson.com/2026/08/31/on-not-becoming-a-cyborg/","score":21,"flags":0,"comment_count":2,"description":"","description_plain":"","submitter_user":"cgrind